<decompilertest>
<binaryimage arch="MIPS:LE:32:default:default">
<!--
    MIPS o32 is ILP32: long is 4 bytes and long long is 8. mips32le.cspec
    stated no long_size, so the type factory fell back to 8 bytes (its LP64
    default when int is 4). A declared long then took two registers, and an
    8-byte value printed as long instead of long long:
        int8 ln(int8 a) { return CONCAT44(v1,(int4)a + 1); }           (bug)
        int4 cl(unsigned int a0,unsigned int a1)
          { return (int4)ln(CONCAT44(a1,a0)) * 3; }                     (bug)
        unsigned long cll(unsigned int a0,unsigned int a1)              (bug)
    Stage: P0 knowledge plane (compiler-spec data organization). No option:
    the cspec states the ABI's long_size, a data fix.

    Source (mipsel-linux-gnu-gcc 10.3 -O2 -fno-pic -mno-abicalls -nostdlib
    -static -Wl,-Ttext=0x10000 -Wl,-e,_start; .text verbatim below):
        __attribute__((noinline)) long ln(long a) { return a + 1; }
        __attribute__((noinline)) long long ll(long long a) { return a + 1; }
        __attribute__((noinline)) long pick(long a, long long b, long c)
          { return a + c + (long)b; }
        long cl(long x) { return ln(x) * 3; }
        long long cll(long long x) { return ll(x) * 3; }
        long cp(long a, long long b) { return pick(a, b, 7) * 2; }
        void _start(void) {}
    pick reads a in a0, b in the aligned pair a2:a3 (low word a2) and c from
    the first stack argument slot, 16(sp).

    Pass 1 (catalog default) decompiles cll before anything is declared, then
    declares ln, ll and pick with C keyword types and decompiles the rest.
    Pass 2 repeats ln, ll and pick with option ctypes on (the CLI rendering).
    Assert #1: cll's recovered 8-byte return and local spell long long.
    Assert #2: a declared long is 4 bytes; ln adds one to a single register.
    Assert #3: pick takes a 4-byte a and c around the 8-byte b.
    Assert #4: pick and cl/cp's calls use one register per long.
    Assert #5: with ctypes on the prototypes read int / long long.
    Assert #6: no high word is invented for a long, and nothing spells an
    8-byte integer as unsigned long.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x10000" readonly="true">
